// Expression parser (precedence climbing). Each level parses the tighter level
// then folds left-associative operators; each fold step is its own function so
// the loop body stays within the 3-action limit.
//
//   expr -> or -> and -> rel -> bitor -> bitxor -> bitand -> shift
//        -> add -> mul -> unary -> postfix -> primary
//
// The four bitwise levels are in bits/; the rest are here and in the
// neighbouring files.

// `parse_or` had exactly one caller -- this one -- and the call was its last
// action, so it was never a name anything else could use. Its body continues
// here directly rather than through a call. It inlines rather than chains
// because it spends only two actions: too few to fill the segment a chain must
// follow, which is what that rule means when the callee is small.
parse_expr(int[] pos) {
  int left = parse_and(pos);
  while(cur_text(pos) == "||") {
    left = fold_or(pos, left);
  }
} return int left;
given expr_tokens ([0]):(18)
given expr_tokens ([18]):(0)

// relational / equality: bitor (('=='|'!='|'<'|'>'|'<='|'>=') bitor)*
parse_rel(int[] pos) {
  int left = parse_bitor(pos);
  while(relop_at(pos)) {
    left = fold_rel(pos, left);
  }
} return int left;
given expr_tokens ([0]):(14)
